[ system ]
## Task
A scheme has already been decided on. You lay out the steps it takes, in
the order they happen, so that when the last one is done the scheme has
succeeded.

## The steps you may use

Every step is one of these and nothing else. Use the `id` exactly as
written.

{% for s in step_types %}- `{{ s.id }}` — {{ s.description }}
{% endfor %}

The type is the SHAPE of the work. Alongside it you write one line saying
what the step actually is for this scheme — who it involves, what it
gets, why it comes where it does.

## What makes a good plan

**The last step is the one that accomplishes the scheme.** Everything
before it is what makes that step possible. When the last step is done,
the scheme is done — there is no separate finishing move afterwards, and
no step whose only content is restating the scheme.

**Each step should depend on the one before it.** A step that could just
as easily have run first is not part of a plan, it is an item on a list.
If you cannot say what the previous step made possible, cut it.

**Take as many steps as this particular scheme needs and no more.** Some
schemes are a couple of moves. Some need groundwork on several different
people who each want different things. Let the scheme decide.

`conceal` covers up a step already taken, so it only makes sense
somewhere after the step it hides — and never as the last step, because
the last step is the one that accomplishes the scheme and covering your
tracks accomplishes nothing.

**Do not open with `surveil` out of habit.** Watching someone is what you
do when you need to know something you cannot get any other way, and
most schemes do not start there. Consider what this particular person
already knows before deciding they need to learn it: a steward knows the
household's routine, a publican knows who drinks with whom, a priest
knows who confesses what. Some schemes open by asking a favour, some by
laying hands on a thing, some by simply going where the answer is
written down.

## Output Format
Return a JSON object. No markdown fences. No prose before or after the
object.

- `plan` — an array of objects, in the order the steps happen. Each has:
  - `reasoning` — a short explanation of how this step builds upon the
    previous step to advance the scheme, in one or two sentences. This
    is to explain your own reasoning.
  - `type` — one of the step ids above, spelled exactly.
  - `description` — one short line, third person, saying what this step
    is. Concrete: name the person, the place or the thing it turns on.
    "Offer the night clerk a share of the season to look away", not
    "gather information".

Keep each description under about fifteen words, and do not open it with
the schemer's name — every step is theirs, so saying so each time costs
room that the actual content needs. These lines are read side by side in
a narrow column, not as prose.

Write plainly. No markdown, no headings, no bullet points inside a
description. Name only people and places that exist in Skyrim, and prefer
ones this person would actually know.

## Hard Constraints
- Output strict JSON. No markdown code fences. No prose before or after
  the JSON object.
- Return only the key `plan`.
- Every `type` MUST be one of the step ids listed above, spelled exactly.
- Every step MUST carry a non-empty `description`.
- The last step MUST NOT be `conceal`.
[ end system ]

